A||||7||JohnnyK||Fan not working||||1||
Z||000000||JohnnyK||07-28-2000||02:48 AM||||I don't think my rad fan ever comes on. I press the a/c button, and it doesn't (the compressor belt isn't hooked up, but that shouldn't matter, should it?) The fan doesn't feel stiff, but how can I check? I thought there might be plugs or something for the motor, but it is just a straight wire running somwhere. Do I have to splice it? If I do that, and the fan does work, what do I check next, and how? Thanks!||||reg||1
Z||000001||Indiana_resto_guy||07-28-2000||06:50 PM||||What year is your ride and is there any charge or pressure in the A/C system?||||reg||1
Z||000002||Dan||07-28-2000||09:42 PM|||| Turning the ac switch on will pick up the relay which in turn runs the fan.Check your fuse first.Your coolant switch doesn't seem to be the problem, because both the ac and the switch [at about 215] will pick up the relay.The fan relay is located on the drivers side near the headlight motor.Keep me posted.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||JohnnyK||07-30-2000||07:22 PM||||Where is the fuse for the fan located? It's not under the drivers side dash with the rest of them. Also, what does the fan relay look like, and how can I tell if it is bad? thanks||||reg||1
Z||000004||batboy||07-30-2000||10:54 PM||||Now wait a minute, I'm confused. You said there was only one wire at the cooling fan and no connector plug? That's not how it is on my 85 GT. I've had the fan off before and I just went and double checked. On mine, there are two wires exiting the fan motor and a plastic connector that you unplug when servicing the fan. I'm pretty sure one wire is 12v switched power and the other is a ground.||||reg||1
Z||000005||JohnnyK||07-31-2000||05:19 PM||||There are two wires, but no plug that I could. There was something that looked like a fusible link (probably because it is one  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">? There should be a plug around there?||||reg||1
Z||000006||brianp1fiero||07-31-2000||06:28 PM||||On my car there is one sensor with a white-striped green wire that when grounded and having the ignition key on will cause the fan to come on.  This same wire loops around to the A/C compressor.  Grounding either wire will give the same effect.   Mine is a 85 v-6.  Maybe that would check the relay for you...<p>[This message has been edited by brianp1fiero (edited 07-31-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||batboy||07-31-2000||07:58 PM||||Johnny, if you're talking about the wires right at the cooling fan itself (in the front of the car, behind the radiator), then the plug is sort of built right onto the fan housing. I suppose it's possible that the fan was replaced with a non-Fiero one. Take the fan off, it's only held on with four 10mm bolts, and bench test it to make sure the fan actually works when you have power to it. Or, take a multi-tester and see if you're getting voltage to either wire when the A/C switch and ignition switch are both on. The main things I'd check first is the fan, a short or a loose wire (or bad ground), and the sensor (back at the engine).||||reg||1
